# GymOS — environment template.
#
#   cp .env.example .env      (Windows: Copy-Item .env.example .env)
#
# ── You do not need this file to develop ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `docker compose up -d` works from a clean clone with no .env at all: docker-compose.override.yml
# carries development defaults for everything, and they are deliberately public. Copy this file
# only when you want to change something — a different port, JSON logs, a real secret.
#
# ── .env is gitignored, and must stay that way ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Compose loads .env automatically, which makes it the natural place for a real credential and
# therefore the natural place to leak one. Never commit it. Nothing in *this* file is sensitive:
# every value below is either a placeholder or a development default.
#
# Variable names mirror the application's own configuration keys (ASP.NET maps
# Security__Jwt__SigningKey to Security:Jwt:SigningKey), so there is one naming scheme, not two.
# Full reference: docs/Infrastructure/DOCKER.md.

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# SQL Server
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# The database name, and the one place the environment is visible at a glance.
#
# GymOS has exactly three databases, one per environment, and they are named differently on purpose:
#
#   GymOS_Local        this file's default — the local Docker sidecar, per developer
#   GymOS_UAT          the shared Development/Staging deployment
#   GymOS_Production    Production, and nothing else
#
# Distinct names rather than one name on three servers, because a name is checkable. The application
# validates at startup that the environment and the database agree (Development may only open
# GymOS_Local, Staging only GymOS_UAT, Production only GymOS_Production) and refuses to boot on a
# mismatch, so a copied connection string fails immediately instead of quietly writing to the wrong
# environment. docker-compose.prod.yml makes this variable REQUIRED for the same reason: production
# must never inherit a default.
GYMOS_DB=GymOS_Local

# The `sa` login the image creates — for humans and ad hoc maintenance only. Neither the API nor
# migrations ever connect as it, and that is a correctness requirement rather than hygiene:
# sysadmin and db_owner hold ALTER ANY SECURITY POLICY, so a connection using either could switch
# R14's tenant isolation off in one statement, and TenantIsolationValidator refuses to boot such a
# host. docker/sqlserver/initdb/10-principals.sql creates the two principals the application
# actually uses (see the two passwords below and docs/BusinessRules/TenantIsolation.md).
#
# SQL Server enforces password complexity — 8+ characters, three of upper/lower/digit/symbol — and
# refuses to start at all if the sa password fails it. That is why these development values are
# longer and mixed-case than the PostgreSQL ones they replace.
MSSQL_SA_PASSWORD=Gymos_dev_sa_password1

# Read by docker/sqlserver/initdb/10-principals.sql, applied by the one-shot `db-init` service.
# Unlike the PostgreSQL scripts these replace, they are idempotent and run on every `up` rather
# than only against an empty data directory — so changing a password here and re-running
# `docker compose up -d` will NOT rotate it. Rotate with ALTER LOGIN, or `docker compose down -v`
# for a throwaway environment.
#
# gymos_migrator owns the schemas and every object migrations create, and is the only principal
# that runs DDL — used solely by the one-shot `migrator` service (docker-compose.yml). Development
# default supplied by the override file; production REQUIRES this.
GYMOS_MIGRATOR_PASSWORD=Gymos_dev_migrator_password1

# gymos_app owns nothing and holds no DDL or security-administration rights, so it cannot disable a
# security policy even given an SQL injection — the whole reason the API and the migrator are
# different principals. This is what the API connects as at runtime. Development default supplied
# by the override file; production REQUIRES this.
GYMOS_APP_PASSWORD=Gymos_dev_app_password1

# Host port for the database. Change it if you already run SQL Server or LocalDB locally — a native
# install wins the race for 1433 and the symptom is a login failure, not a port conflict. The API is
# unaffected either way: it reaches the database over the compose network.
MSSQL_PORT=1433

# Edition. Developer is free for non-production use and feature-identical to Enterprise, which is
# what makes table partitioning and row-level security behave in development exactly as they do on
# Azure SQL. Production REQUIRES this to be set to a licensed edition.
MSSQL_PID=Developer

# Development only. The sidecar presents a self-signed certificate, so verification cannot succeed
# against it; the override file turns this on. Production leaves it off and verifies for real — an
# encrypted connection that trusts any certificate is not an authenticated one.
MSSQL_TRUST_SERVER_CERTIFICATE=true

# Production only. The container publishes nothing; these describe an external managed instance
# (Azure SQL Database, AD-11) when you stop running SQL Server in a container. Apply
# docs/runbooks/managed-sqlserver-roles.md to it once — Azure SQL has no `sa` and no server-scoped
# CREATE LOGIN, so the initdb scripts do not run there unchanged.
#MSSQL_HOST=gymos-db.database.windows.net
#MSSQL_PORT_INTERNAL=1433

# GYMOS_DB_CONNECTION is GONE, and its absence is the point.
#
# It used to hold a full connection string for an external shared development database, and
# docker-compose.override.yml honoured it in place of the local sidecar. That is the one thing the
# environment model does not allow: local development uses GymOS_Local, the Development/Staging
# deployment uses GymOS_UAT, production uses GymOS_Production, and nothing local reaches either of
# the other two. A variable whose whole job was to redirect the local stack at a shared database
# defeated that, invisibly, from a gitignored file.
#
# If you have an old `.env` that still sets it, delete the line — it is now ignored, so it will not
# break the stack, but it also will not do what it used to. If it held a PostgreSQL or Neon
# connection string, that credential should be rotated: it is stale either way.

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# GymOS Web API
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Host port. 5080 matches `dotnet run`, so URLs work whichever way the API is running.
API_PORT=5080

# Production: the image CI built and tested. Local development builds from source instead.
#API_IMAGE=ghcr.io/gymos-management/gymos-api:0.7.0

# Production: which interface to publish on. Loopback by default, because only a reverse proxy on
# the same host should reach the API directly.
#API_BIND=127.0.0.1

# Development | Staging | Production. Development is what makes the stack start with no secrets:
# it loads the deliberately public signing key from appsettings.Development.json.
#ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Development

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Secrets — placeholders only
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# REQUIRED outside Development, where startup validates it is at least 32 bytes and refuses to
# boot otherwise. Generate a unique one per environment:
#     openssl rand -base64 48
# Leave empty for local development.
GYMOS_JWT_SIGNING_KEY=

GYMOS_JWT_ISSUER=gymos-backend
GYMOS_JWT_AUDIENCE=gymos-clients

# The first administrator, created once and never reset. Development defaults to
# admin@gymos.local / gymos-dev-admin-password so a clean clone has a working sign-in. In any real
# environment set these for the first deployment only, then unset them.
GYMOS_SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@gymos.local
GYMOS_SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD=gymos-dev-admin-password

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Behaviour
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# true only when a reverse proxy genuinely sits in front. Without it the rate limiter sees one
# bucket for every caller and audit rows all record the proxy's address; with it and no proxy, a
# client can spoof its own address.
GYMOS_FORWARDED_HEADERS_ENABLED=false

# ── GYMOS_MIGRATE_ON_STARTUP is deliberately absent ───────────────────────────────────────────
# It used to set Database__MigrateOnStartup on the API container, and an older version of this file
# shipped it as `true`. Migrations are now the one-shot `migrator` service's job in every environment
# (docker-compose.yml, connecting as gymos_migrator), and the API connects as gymos_app, which holds
# no DDL rights — so a stale .env carrying the old `true` would have made the API attempt DDL it
# cannot perform on every boot. docker-compose.yml now hard-codes `false` with no variable behind it,
# which makes any leftover value inert; this note exists so the variable is not helpfully restored.
# See DOCKER.md §2 ("gymos-migrator") and §6 ("Production workflow").

# json | text. Empty means environment-derived: text in Development, JSON everywhere else. Set
# json locally to see exactly what a log collector will receive.
GYMOS_LOG_FORMAT=

# Verbose | Debug | Information | Warning | Error | Fatal
GYMOS_LOG_LEVEL=Information

# OTLP collector endpoint (AD-38). Empty disables export; nothing else needs changing to enable
# tracing. With the observability profile running: http://seq:5341/ingest/otlp/v1/traces
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Optional services — inert until their profile is enabled
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
#   (the `tools` profile is now empty — pgAdmin was its only service and cannot speak to SQL Server)
#   docker compose --profile redis up -d           Redis
#   docker compose --profile observability up -d   Seq
#   docker compose --profile storage up -d         MinIO

MAILPIT_SMTP_PORT=1025
MAILPIT_UI_PORT=8025

REDIS_PORT=6379

# Seq's UI is on 80 inside the container; 8341 on the host keeps it clear of the API.
# 5341 is the ingestion endpoint, published so a host-run `dotnet run` can log into it too.
SEQ_UI_PORT=8341
SEQ_INGEST_PORT=5341

# Seq refuses to start without an administrator password. Sign in as `admin`.
SEQ_ADMIN_PASSWORD=gymos-dev-seq

# MinIO refuses to start without a root password of at least 8 characters.
MINIO_ROOT_USER=gymos
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=gymos-dev-minio
MINIO_API_PORT=9000
MINIO_CONSOLE_PORT=9001

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Resource limits — production overlay only
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Sized for a small single-host deployment rather than chosen by measurement. Raise them once you
# have numbers.
#API_MEMORY_LIMIT=1g
#MSSQL_MEMORY_LIMIT=4g
